  "worked_solution": "We must find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-2=0.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\u2207f(x,y)=\\lambda \u2207g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=0\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u2207f(x,y) & =\\lambda \u2207g(x,y) \\\\ \u27e8{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)\u27e9 & =\\lambda \u27e8{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)\u27e9 \\\\ \u27e82x,2y+4\u27e9 & =\\lambda \u27e82x,4y\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2x=2\\lambda x \\\\ 2y+4=4\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,\u21d2\\,{\\begin{aligned}x(1-\\lambda )=0 \\\\ y(2\\lambda -1)=2\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition ${x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-2=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{y}^{2}-2 & =0\\,\u21d2\\,y=\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,-1),(0,1).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =1$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation of the system implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(2(1)-1)=2\\,\u21d2\\,y=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the constraint condition ${x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-2=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}=-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives a contradiction.\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,-1),\\,(0,1).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,-1)$ | $(0,1)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $1$ | $9$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at the point $(0,1)$ and its maximum value is $9.$",
